  <static seed="2119" source="Contrib/PCC/BasicAlgebra/NumberBasics/TypesOfNumbers10.pg">
    <statement><p>Which of the following are whole numbers?  There may be more than
one correct answer.</p>
<p><var form="checkboxes">
<li>
<p><m>42</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>0</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>\pi</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>0.101001000100001\ldots</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>-6</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>3.6\overline{75}</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>2.117</m></p>
</li>
<li>
<p><m>-16725</m></p>
</li>
</var></p>
</statement>
    <solution><p>To be a whole number, you have to be one of the numbers <m>0,1,2,\ldots</m>. Sometimes whole numbers are explicitly written this way, and sometimes they are hidden. For example <m>\sqrt{4}</m> is a whole number, since <m>\sqrt{4}=2</m>. So the correct answers are AB.</p>
</solution>
